//! run
//! abort
//! stderr: <runtime>:0:0: runtime error: invalid release
//! stdout:
//| after the first free
// `rt.free` releases an allocation with no position of a `del`: the compiler that builds this
// one emits a call of it. A second `rt.free` of one allocation is the runtime error `invalid
// release` at `<runtime>:0:0`, because the address starts no live allocation (D10.9 rule 4).
// The function tests the address and reads no byte of the dead storage.
// D10.3, D10.9
import extern;
import std.rt;

string FILE = "055_invalid_release_rt_free.ft";

// C call rt.alloc: &FILE[0] keeps the bound of the string (D9.8 rule 2), and its NUL is the NUL
// of the literal that initializes the constant (rule 4).
// C call rt.free x2: p, the block of 32 bytes that rt.alloc gave. The second call gets the
// address of dead storage, and rt.free reads no byte at it.
fn main() i32 {
    void mut* p = rt.alloc(1, 32, &FILE[0], 21, 19);
    rt.free(p);
    println("after the first free");
    rt.free(p);
    println("after the second free");
    return 0;
}
